{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/112806-multiple-enemies-for-demons","id":112806,"title":"Multiple Enemies for Demons","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Demon: The Descent"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-04-14T11:34:21-07:00","id":112806,"title":"Multiple Enemies for Demons","content":"Other than Demons the Decent and Vampire the Requiem core I do not have any new world of darkness book. My question is how much better is Vampire Blood and Smoke compared to vampire the requiem for players and to run as antagonists for Demons the Decent? Are there any other new WOD creature/core books that would be good to use against Demons? I am trying to build my arsenal of non Angels a bit, I find it best to have 2-3 go to enemy types or else the party gets to used to the combats.<br /> Currently I am thinking Vampires and posibly Promethians or Werewolves. Note these are not going to be allied groups just options to use so not every enemy is human or angels.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2733-d-acolyte","name":"D acolyte","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-14T12:23:44-07:00","id":112853,"title":"","content":"I'm wondering this myself. I'm thinking wizards would make excellent adversaries, as well as hashmalum (considering they're mildly connected to demon anyways)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1085-oddness","name":"Oddness","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"109"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-14T14:50:59-07:00","id":112943,"title":"","content":"Out of the Book Demon's have. Angels, Stigmatic's, Cryptid's, the hint at Half-Breeds, other Demon's/ Rival cults, and Exile's. Demon is an espionage game so I don't understand &quot;the party gets used to the combats&quot; considering combat isn't something the Demon jumps for joy over. In the upcoming hunter book there's a group of hunter's empowered by angels who act as a Demon antagonist. I honestly can't see why Demon's would interact with any other supernatural. If a say a vampire has figured out a Demon isn't human than the Demon is in way more trouble than just a blood sucker. If it's a fight over territory than it won't really be decided by a down and dirty brawl anyways and you'd be just as well to use one of the established potential enemies a Demon has than going &quot;Lol you're not prepared for these crossover powerz&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1064-miss_minty","name":"miss_minty","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"283"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-15T07:08:03-07:00","id":113298,"title":"","content":"Every campaign at some point is bound to have combat, especially if your players are foolish. If you have a variety of enemy to draw on these combats can be new and interesting. I am less concerned with crossover power then I am the \u201cit is an end to the story, time to kill yet another bane\u201d sort of expression; something I said about a Werewolf the Apocalypse game I played in. Through in some good fluff for the others creatures to be there, such as Werewolfs of the Forsaken hunting down Demons for being renegade spirits or possessed human. This can change an espionage game to a hide and seek sort of game. Another option is that if the party a group of bleeding heart demons then how will they take to vampire eating in their area. <br /> I started with vampires because I tend to think they are good pawns for the god machine in some situations and can be used without the god machine. In Blood and Smoke vampires main enemy is demonically possessed humans (Strix) if I recall, vampires could make a mix up between the two groups or you could have these Strix be sent by the god machine to kill a vampire and the party of Demons has to stop them.<br /> Finally why limit yourself to the standard enemies, just do not go heavy into the other systems. I tend to like kill two birds with one stone, an enemy on story may be something else the next.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2733-d-acolyte","name":"D acolyte","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-15T07:49:37-07:00","id":113322,"title":"","content":"But you're solely looking for other splats to throw at your players. Not &quot;Is this a plot that can't be resolved any other way than throwing a werewolf in there&quot;. There is nothing &quot;Standard&quot; about enemies, if all your antagonists come to the came conclusions via the same means that's not an antagonist problem that's a storyteller problem. But then I'm opposed to crossover at any front I think it's lazy, <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1064-miss_minty","name":"miss_minty","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"283"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-15T08:18:57-07:00","id":113340,"title":"","content":"Miss_minty if this was old world of darkness I would agree that cross overs are not the best idea, but from what I understand they made it work better in new world of darkness. I am not sure how it is lazy if incorporated well, may be a pain in the neck but not lazy.<br /> I am looking for antagonists and enemies not splat or fodder for parties, I tend to build a campaign around character X wants to do Y, so it is important to understand character X from the ground up; this often takes longer then building a character if I am playing instead of running a game.<br /> Most all my games do not come to the same conclusions, but just because I know the differences or the subtle difference in powers does not mean that the party will care. For instance there where a good number of banes in Werewolf the Apocalypse, but the best strategy was always the same: lure them into the real world, surround, and then stab/attack till it is dead. If it had venomous blood meh go with guns, if it shoots eye rays then get close. The difference mattered little and so the fights with banes became repetitive, now that may not be true with the demon antagonists; I have been running Ravenloft (a different foe every 4-5 games) since the kickstarter and only looked through the enemy sections a few times.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2733-d-acolyte","name":"D acolyte","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-15T09:21:56-07:00","id":113371,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure what part of this sounds lazy. Having a game where the demons must infiltrate a group of vampires, or race against time tracking down a mummy before their cover is blown in enemy territory, or figure out how a bunch of giests are somehow the linchpin in a major infrastructure in the area... The stories are there as long as you remember Demon's roots.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1085-oddness","name":"Oddness","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"109"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-15T11:37:42-07:00","id":113449,"title":"","content":"Your question now has me brainstorming an excellent crossover chronicle which I would love to play/run now: Demon / Changeling (and it'd work well from either perspective) [you'd probably want the CtL core and Autumn Nightmares, though]. It'd make an interesting long story arc (particularly if immediate problems popped up in between) of discovering another group, befriending over common goals, and then devolving into terrible enemies.<br /> <br /> On the surface, changelings and demons have a lot in common: they're both hunted by otherworldly beings more powerful than they are, they both try to maintain &quot;covers&quot; amongst humanity, and they're both on personal quests toward freedom / self-acceptance. They'd have a lot they can teach each other in these goals as well, particularly because of the key differences. For example, True Fey are a lot more rare to see than angels and live in a separate realm, but loyalists infiltrating a group is a lot more common; their magic (mostly) doesn't attract attention from their enemies, but when they're hunted its much harder to hide.... If demons and changelings were to trade tactics, it wouldn't be a direct translation, but it would help both groups in their respective spycraft.<br /> <br /> [to help the groups recognize each other initially, I would houserule that Changelings see a Demons' covers as thin veneers of identity, like their own Masks, but with nothing underneath - which does /not/ happen. Demons, watching long enough, would notice changelings trying to maintain a cover while pursuing a supernatural life, and being fairly cavalier about using their &quot;Exploits&quot; and &quot;demonic forms&quot;.]<br /> <br /> This would quickly devolve, of course. Once the changelings learn about the Pacts demons make for Cover, and once demons start thinking of Arcadia as a place to pursue Hell, the changelings would immediately become hostile. And they'd be a super fun antagonist for demons: both groups are sneaky blighters which have to worry about being discovered by their respective Enemies - Changelings have more freedom to cut loose because their Enemy doesn't really take notice to displays of power (unless they're already looking), but Demons, from what I can tell, have more powerful abilities outright. It'd be more like tag or cat-and-mouse than open combat, using tricky shenanigans, crossplans and cats paws, distractions and baiting ....<br /> <br /> In a nutshell, I'd say Changeling has a lot of potential in Demon as sympathetic villains / former allies, and for heavy tactics warfare. It could require being very familiar with both games, though.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Second Chance for<br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=709\" data-tempid=\"temp_709_1572548741915_354\" /><br /> A Beautiful Madness</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/425-seraph-kitty","name":"Seraph Kitty","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar425_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-15T12:10:25-07:00","id":113470,"title":"","content":"And imagine what would happen if their respective enemies come hunting them at once, just as the two groups go to conflict. They may have to team up. What if the Fae and terminator angels can be turned on each other? Or what if they arrive hand in hand, seemingly both drawn to work together (by some weird sympathy between the wyrd and missions)? Can changelings and demons put aside their differences to help each other evade their mutual enemies? Or will they have to fight wars on three fronts, as they fight each other and their dual enemies?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/935-cleverest-of-things","name":"Cleverest of Things","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar935_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"650"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-15T12:29:10-07:00","id":113481,"title":"","content":"I never really thought of changelings.<br /> When I read some of Demon the Decent and knowing that all/most of NWOD use the WOD core book and there book my mind jumped to here: seeing the GM and his angels as manipulators of humans and others, so what others would be good to vary from story to story and what do I need to run them. I latched onto vampire and werewolves. Those who plot often become pawns in another plot and the way word hunters that might accidently stumble into a spider\u2019s web (yaa Weaver reference). I am going to focus on the Demon the Decent line still, cannot wait for my copy of the hard back to come in.<br /> <br /> Hunter may also be good.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2733-d-acolyte","name":"D acolyte","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-15T14:24:54-07:00","id":113557,"title":"","content":"[Edit: Sorry about the format; my Chromebook doesn't like these forums much...]<br /> <br /> In terms of running a purely Demon the Descent / God Machine chronicle, vampires as a secondary template probably integrate the easiest; as they're heavily focused on human cities and power structures as part of their own survival and politics, they easily can become huge thorns and powerful (unwitting) pawns to the God Machine. I'd probably use them as a pretty powerful force which require a delicate touch: when the GM is subtle, it can use them to accomplish A Lot, but as soon as they sense a direct hand doing something Not-Their-Agenda, they resist and quite effectively undermine the unwitting human infrastructure, just because they're paranoid and petty.<br /> <br /> With werewolves, I would approach from the perspective of &quot;the God Machine wants the world to be Dark&quot; and &quot;the God Machine harnesses Essence for its occult matrices&quot;, and relating it to the spirit world. From the first part, the GM seeks to let spirits into the world, makes sure their are hosts and claimed (and werewolves) preying upon humanity and making the world dark, dangerous, and miserable. From the second part, the GM also seeks to exploit spirits and the spirit world for its own designs. I would play the werewolves as mostly unaware that another force is messing with things: the occult matrices don't make impacts on the Shadow and angels leave them alone; but perhaps a pack clues in that a lot of loci/verges keep appearing, the hosts seem oddly well equipped and aided by the city government, spirits sometimes get messages from angels (or kidnapped by them). They could be unwitting elimination infrastructure or a factor of chaos (both to demons and to the God Machine), with the potential of a tight-rope thin alliance, if you can sit down and talk with them.<br /> <br /> Changeling is one of my favorite gamelines, though, and I think it has -tons- of crossover potential with Demon. In addition to my above scenario, Changelings have one myth that the True Fey are &quot;half fallen&quot; angels - those that didn't take sides between heaven and hell [and if you make a relationship between Titles and Cover, there may be enough truth to tempt a demon into pursue Hell straight into Arcadia]. Changeling also introduces a force which could potentially rival the God Machine; the Wyrd is the tangled skein of fate, a narrative force which weaves Stories out of peoples' lives. It mostly affects the fae-touched, isn't sentient (we think), and doesn't have any motives which interfere with the God Machine, but it could possibly become a force to rival the GM if a cult of changelings and demons tries the right things - and if it doesn't warp out of control and spiral everyone's lives into madness first.<br /> <br /> &quot;Second Sight&quot; I might also recommend as supplemental figures for a Demon game. Off the top of my head, Psychics being a consequence of the GM's workings and its occasional tools; Thaumaturges being able to study Occult Matrices and design their own rituals from it; Cults variably serving beings that could be greater servants of the GM or rival gods that it banished -- those are pretty stereotypical roles, and far from the full potential in that book, but.<br /> <br /> Yeah those are the first gamelines I would look at to make a demon game more dynamic and convoluted and expansive. Mummy sounds like it has great potential too; I'm not very familiar with it but Oddness' idea sounded awesome, and I bet the Judges and the GM have a contentious history behind the curtains of the world. For me, though, Changeling wins in terms of crossover potential.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">Second Chance for<br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=709\" data-tempid=\"temp_709_1572548741915_354\" /><br /> A Beautiful Madness</div>","edited":{"userid":425,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/425-seraph-kitty","user":"Seraph Kitty","date":". 04-15-2014, 08:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/425-seraph-kitty","name":"Seraph Kitty","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar425_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-16T14:25:43-07:00","id":114329,"title":"","content":"If you DO use werewolf, I love the idea of Weaver references. I went so far as to bring back pattern spiders (as spirits &quot;infected&quot; with God, sorta like how demons in Inferno can be spirits or ghosts infected with evil). I also have Azlu as unwitting pawns of the GM, sealing up the gauntlet in places &quot;Mother Spider&quot; needs spirits kept out of.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/935-cleverest-of-things","name":"Cleverest of Things","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar935_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"650"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-16T14:58:32-07:00","id":114348,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cleverest of Things</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/112806-multiple-enemies-for-demons?p=114329#post114329\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you DO use werewolf, I love the idea of Weaver references. I went so far as to bring back pattern spiders (as spirits &quot;infected&quot; with God, sorta like how demons in Inferno can be spirits or ghosts infected with evil). I also have Azlu as unwitting pawns of the GM, sealing up the gauntlet in places &quot;Mother Spider&quot; needs spirits kept out of.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Rage notes that the Azlu are particularly numerous on the West Coast, and Seattle is singled out as a place where they've raised the Gauntlet especially high; also, the text of Fortify the Border Marches makes reference to &quot;the spider spirits that skitter along the Gauntlet itself&quot; and &quot;the spirits that maintain the Gauntlet&quot; and further specifically describes the rite as mimicking the abilities of the Azlu to a lessened extent.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-01T20:27:09-07:00","id":156192,"title":"","content":"Sorry to reup the thread, but I saw this a while ago, and wanted to say what I had to say correctly.<br /> <br /> William Wohlforth would call Vanilla Demon: the Descent a &quot;unipolar world&quot;. The G-M is the sole superpower in the setting and is without equal. Compare this with Werewolf: the Forsaken, where the Uratha have deal with multiple threats on multiple fronts. (The upcoming Idigram Chonicles takes this and runs with it) There are other threats in the main DtD book (Stigmatics, Cryptids, Sleepers) as well other imported splats that are a possible threat (hunters anyone?) but none are the howling, existential threat that is the G-M.<br /> <br /> Demon is storytelling game. When you add something to a game, you have to within the rules of story and it has to give you new ways to tell the story. If you want a a new enemy, it should resonate with the existing themes of DtD. It should serve as a foil to both the characters, and to the G-M, which is very much a character in the native; the G-M is the whip that drive DtD.<br /> <br /> Themes:<br /> Technognosis. Mystical insight is the objective. The characters are castoffs thrown into a world that does not make sense. They have to look at the machine within and the machine without to obtain knowledge of themselves.<br /> <br /> Espionage: Tradecraft is the means. The characters are spies, their ability to function depends on secrecy, subterfuge and guile. Why do this? Partially because of the nature of there abilities (lying, cover, etc), but more than that is the disparity in power. even the most powerful Unchained can be brought down.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>miss_minty</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/demon-the-descent/112806-multiple-enemies-for-demons?p=112943#post112943\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Demon is an espionage game so I don't understand &quot;the party gets used to the combats&quot; considering combat isn't something the Demon jumps for joy over.</div> </div> </div> </div>Perfect. Demons operate through stealth and preparation. Our new antagonists need to reinforce this, we want organizations that operate at tier 3, even if the players engage them at Tier 1, maybe Tier 2 for chapter climaxes. <br /> <br /> So:<br /> <u><b>Recommended threat 1: The Seers of the Throne.</b></u> In contrast to the G-M, these &quot;mere&quot; humans. In contrast the unchained, the SotT wield vast (but hidden) power over reality, both through magic, and through vast economics and political pulling strings. And... these guys want it all; there is no enough, only more. This contrasts with Demons' own search for a purpose and meaning, these guys now exactly what they want and are will hurt anyone to get it, and their raison d'etre is to deny enlightenment to others, <br /> <br /> In G-M chronicle, the obvious new goal of the SotT to usurp the G-M. And they just might be able to pull it off. <br /> <br /> Worse, they understand human motivations in ways that the G-M and the unchained can't. The G-M can recruit human servitors, and understands that person X plus bribe Y equals desired outcome Z, but is ignorant of the mathematics that drive that particular equations. The Demons have defected to the human race but they're not human, and often have very different needs and thought processes.<br /> <br /> The SotT play foil to both the Inquisitors and the Tempters. The Watchers information, the Seers have it. The Decadents want temporal power, the Seers have it. Furthermore, the question of how such an organization has been able to operate for however long under the G-M\u2019s nose is something that both agendas will want to answer. <br /> <br /> I recommend re-skinning them when your players run into them, ( say the Illuminati), or better yet give them layers and layers of masks. This is an espionage game, the players should not have find out about this enemy through their sourcebook on drivethruRPG<br /> <br /> <u><b>Recommended threat 2</b></u>: Go outside the traditional WoD and steal <u><b>Nemesis</b></u> from Jim Butcher\u2019s <i>Dresden Files</i>. Brief rundown: Nemesis is an entity that infiltrates the minds of victims and turns them to the service of wiping out all reality at the behest of the Outsiders. In short, it hollows out souls and wears them as suits. Does this remind you of anyone?<br /> <br /> In addition to that hopefully upsetting bit of symmetry, Nemesis is good foil to the G-M. The G-M is mechanical and seeks to preserve the status quo, Nemesis is organic and seeks nihilistic annihilation.<br /> <br /> It also shakes up the dynamic between Integrators and the other agendas. In vanilla DtD the Integrators are regarded with barely concealed condescension, contempt, and distrust by other agendas. Hell, their agenda beat condition is \u201cMake the rest of your ring distrust you.\u201d Give the game a second existential threat in opposition to the G-M and all of sudden, the other agendas are forced to consider \u201cwhat if these guys are right? What if the G-M is the necessary evil?\u201d For Saboteurs, their objective is now longer something regarded as a quixotic goal that most of the agendas are indifferent to. \u201cWhat if they pull it off? What then?\u201d<br /> <br /> Add both of these threats and players are no longer behind enemy lines, they are behind enemy lines in the middle of a war.<br /> <br /> Hope that helps. Happy Storytelling.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2233-agentwestmer","name":"Agentwestmer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2233_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"394"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-01T21:42:51-07:00","id":156246,"title":"","content":"The Unchained have to deal with Angels, rival groups of Unchained, cultists and Stigmatics in thrall to the Machine, Cryptids, and the Daeva Corporation. <i>Hunter: Mortal Remains</i> adds Utopia Now as rivals and the Knights of St. Adrian as direct antagonists. If you're particularly intolerant (or the individual in question is aggressive in their beliefs), then Integrators might not get along well with the group, and neither would their Exile kin. If you run with the God-Machine as the demiurge to <i>Promethean</i>'s Principle as True Divinity, then Qashmallim might have beef with them. That's plenty of antagonists right there.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-06-01T23:36:47-07:00","id":156285,"title":"","content":"One hardly needs to go outside the world of darkness for lovecraftian horrors, there's an entire chapter in second sight for cults that worship reality shattering nihilist lovecraftian horrors","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/391-stsword","name":"StSword","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar391_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1635"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}